[{:rule-id "clojure", :item {:feed-id "planet-clojure", :item-id "https://tarnbarford.net/journal/vimslime", :title "vim-slime", :link "https://tarnbarford.net/journal/vimslime", :published-at #inst "2026-11-02T14:43:27.000-00:00", :content "<!DOCTYPE html> <html> <head> <title>Tarn Barford</title> <meta charset=\"utf-8\"/> <link rel=\"icon\" type=\"image/x-icon\" href=\"/favicon.ico\"> <link href=\"/style.css\" media=\"screen\" rel=\"stylesheet\" type=\"text/css\" /> <link rel=\"alternate\" type=\"application/atom+xml\" title=\"Journals of Tarn Barford\" href=\"/atom\" /> <link href=\"/highlight.css\" media=\"screen\" rel=\"stylesheet\" type=\"text/css\" /> <link href=\"/highlight-console.css\" media=\"screen\" rel=\"stylesheet\" type=\"text/css\" /> </head> <body> <div id=\"container\"> <div id=\"header\"> <div id=\"header\"> <p>From the <a href=\"/journal\">Journals</a> of <a href=\"/\">Tarn Barford</a></p> <h1> vim-slime </h1> <p> Mar 26, 2012 </p> </div> </div> <div id=\"post_content\"> <html><body><p>Today I found the awesomeness that is <a href=\"https://github.com/jpalardy/vim-slime\">vim-slime</a>, it's been an exciting day for me. <a href=\"http://common-lisp.net/project/slime/\">Slime</a> is the \"The Superior Lisp Interaction Mode for Emacs\", I can almost hear the emacs crowd laughing.</p> <p>For those that use vim and haven't used Slime, vim-slime or <a href=\"https://github.com/vim-scripts/VimClojure\">something similar</a>, this is why it's awesome:</p> <p><strong>Text can be sent from any process to the stdin of a <a href=\"http://www.gnu.org/software/screen/\">gnu screen</a> or <a href=\"http://tmux.sourceforge.net/\">tmux</a> session. The process in this case is vim and the screen/tmux session is a terminal</strong>.</p> <p>Screen is a <a href=\"/journal/oh-screen-where-have-you-been\">really neat</a> terminal multiplexer (you can run multiple terminals in a terminal window). The multiplexed shell processes are children of the screen process, which itself is not a child of the terminal window process. This means a screen process and its child processes keep running if you close the terminal window. Later you can re-connect to it, this is what makes vim-slime possible.</p> <p>Here is an screen shot, on the left is me in gVim writing some awful Clojure <a href=\"#footnote-1\">[1]</a>. On the right is a screen buffer in which I started a Clojure REPL. When I want to try run some code I can send any vim text selection to the REPL in a keystroke (or two).</p> <p><img alt=\"vim slime screenshot\" src=\"screenshot.jpg\"/></p> <p>It doesn't have to be a Clojure REPL either, we can send anything to a screen shell. We could run git commands, find, grep, sed, etc. Like with the Clojure REPL we can even interact with any terminal programs that use STDIN.</p> <p>This concept can be taken even further, You can even connect to a tmux session over SSH and share a terminal or a <a href=\"http://remotepairprogramming.com/remote-pair-programming-with-tmux-and-vim-the\">terminal program like vim to do remote pairing</a>!</p> <p>Hopefully remote pairing is the topic of my next post as there are a couple geographically distant people I know who are keen to do some pair hacking. I stand to learn a lot!</p> <p><a name=\"footnote-1\">[1]</a> I learnt almost everything I know about Lisp from <a href=\"http://www.ccs.neu.edu/home/matthias/BTLS/\">The Little Schemer</a>. Great book.</p></body></html> </div> <div id=\"comments\"> </div> </div> <div id=\"footer\"> <p>&nbsp;</p> <p>Questions, comments, suggestions? Email me, <a href=\"mailto:tarn@tarnbarford.net\">tarn@tarnbarford.net</a> (<a href=\"/pgp.txt\">public key</a>)</p> <p>&nbsp;</p> </div> </body> </html>"}, :excerpts [{:text "... or <a href=\"https://github.com/vim-scripts/VimClojure\">something similar</a>, this is why it's...", :matched-terms ["clojure"], :source :content} {:text "... on the left is me in gVim writing some awful Clojure <a href=\"#footnote-1\">[1]</a>. On the right is a screen buffer in which I started a Clojure REPL. When I want to try run some code I can send any vim text selection to the REPL in a keystroke (or two).</p> <p><img alt=\"vim...", :matched-terms ["clojure" "repl"], :source :content} {:text "... <p>It doesn't have to be a Clojure REPL either, we can send anything to a screen shell. We could run git commands, find, grep, sed, etc. Like with the Clojure REPL we can even interact with any terminal programs...", :matched-terms ["clojure" "repl" "command"], :source :content}]} {:rule-id "rescript", :item {:feed-id "planet-clojure", :item-id "https://tarnbarford.net/journal/swipe", :title "Swipe Keyboard", :link "https://tarnbarford.net/journal/swipe", :published-at #inst "2026-11-02T14:43:27.000-00:00", :content "<!DOCTYPE html> <html> <head> <title>Tarn Barford</title> <meta charset=\"utf-8\"/> <link rel=\"icon\" type=\"image/x-icon\" href=\"/favicon.ico\"> <link href=\"/style.css\" media=\"screen\" rel=\"stylesheet\" type=\"text/css\" /> <link rel=\"alternate\" type=\"application/atom+xml\" title=\"Journals of Tarn Barford\" href=\"/atom\" /> <link href=\"/highlight.css\" media=\"screen\" rel=\"stylesheet\" type=\"text/css\" /> <link href=\"/highlight-console.css\" media=\"screen\" rel=\"stylesheet\" type=\"text/css\" /> <style> #swipe-canvas { position: relative; width: 900px; height: 300px; } #swipe-results { font-size: 30px; padding-left: 50px; padding-left: 50px; } #swipe-results ul { margin: 0px; padding: 0px; } #swipe-results li { float: left; background-color: #DDDDDD; list-style-type: none; padding: 10px; margin: 5px; border-radius: 5px; } #swipe { position: relative; } #swipe-loading { position: absolute; height: 50px; width: 300px; top: 85px; left: 300px; background-color: darkgray; border-radius: 10px; text-align: center; padding-top: 20px; border: black; border-width: 5px; } </style> </head> <body> <div id=\"container\"> <div id=\"header\"> <div id=\"header\"> <p>From the <a href=\"/journal\">Journals</a> of <a href=\"/\">Tarn Barford</a></p> <h1> Swipe Keyboard </h1> <p> Apr 06, 2014 </p> </div> </div> <div id=\"post_content\"> <html><body><p>When I first tried a <a href=\"http://www.swype.com/\">Swype</a> keyboard I was impressed how effective it was. Even though I don't use the feature on my phone I was interested in how it could be built, so I <a href=\"https://github.com/tarnacious/swipe-keyboard\">implemented this otherwise useless swipe-able keyboard</a> below. It probably doesn't work on mobile devices, but works on modern browsers with mouse pointers (although I've only really tried Chrome and Firefox).</p> <div id=\"swipe\"> <canvas height=\"300px\" id=\"swipe-canvas\" width=\"900px\"></canvas> <div id=\"swipe-results\"></div> <div style=\"clear: both\"></div> <h2 id=\"swipe-loading\">Loading<noscript>Javascript is Required</noscript></h2> </div> <p>I initially tried to solve this using the technique Peter Norvig famously uses in his <a href=\"http://norvig.com/spell-correct.html]\">spell checker</a>. He takes a sequence of characters and generates a set of word candidates by adding, removing and swapping characters in the original sequence, the generated candidates are removed if they are not found a dictionary. This can work but to be effective too many combinations need to be generated.</p> <p>If the dictionary is indexed into a <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trie\">trie</a> the number of combinations generated can be reduced significantly by traversing the trie and only generating valid letter combinations. This is a pretty bare implementation of that, it requires: </p> <ul> <li>The first and last characters of the initial sequence are used </li> <li>Intermediate characters in the initial sequence can be repeated or discarded </li> <li>No characters are added or swapped</li> </ul> <p>Basically, if you swipe through all the characters in a word in order, then the word will be found if it is in the index regardless how many characters are swiped in between. It is surprisingly quick and effective.</p> <p>This implementation uses <a href=\"https://raw.github.com/first20hours/google-10000-english/master/google-10000-english.txt\">these 10000 words</a>, I intended to use digital books but never got around to it as these words demonstrate the concept well enough.</p> <p>This is the first thing I've written in <a href=\"https://github.com/clojure/clojurescript\">ClojureScript</a> or <a href=\"https://github.com/clojure/clojurescript\">Clojure</a>, so my code my vary from non-idiomatic to shamblolic. I initially used a <a href=\"http://clojuredocs.org/clojure_core/clojure.zip/zipper\">zipper</a> to build the trie with immutable data structures, but found the indexing took to long with my zipper implementation so I <a href=\"https://github.com/tarnacious/swipe-keyboard/commit/6edd7b26e78121fbe8586b3f0ef54ca8277d9e32\">switched to using native Javascript maps</a>.</p> <p>I found that <a href=\"https://github.com/clojure/core.async\">core.async</a> library is really awesome, the <a href=\"http://docs.closure-library.googlecode.com/git/index.html\">Google closure library</a> and <a href=\"https://developers.google.com/closure/compiler/\">compiler</a> integration with <a href=\"http://leiningen.org/\">Leiningen</a> the <a href=\"https://github.com/emezeske/lein-cljsbuild\">cljsbuild plug-in</a> to be impressive. My main pains were the slow JVM start-up time, the advanced closure compiler build of the web worker script fails silently when run (but the main script works fine when compiled with the advanced compiler), and at times I felt some compile time type checking would be nice.</p> <p>I would like to extend this experiment to index the word occurrence counts and proceeding word counts in original text and rank the found words as most likely. Support casing, umlauts, special characters, spelling correction and compound words in the indexing and lookup. I think a live lookup while swiping would also be possible.</p> <p>Overall this was fun, turned out OK I think, and was a great learning experience.</p></body></html> </div> <div id=\"comments\"> </div> </div> <div id=\"footer\"> <p>&nbsp;</p> <p>Questions, comments, suggestions? Email me, <a href=\"mailto:tarn@tarnbarford.net\">tarn@tarnbarford.net</a> (<a href=\"/pgp.txt\">public key</a>)</p> <p>&nbsp;</p> </div> <script src=\"swipe.js\" type=\"text/javascript\"></script> </body> </html>"}, :excerpts [{:text "... <h2 id=\"swipe-loading\">Loading<noscript>Javascript is Required</noscript></h2> </div> <p>I initially...", :matched-terms ["javascript"], :source :content} {:text "... in <a href=\"https://github.com/clojure/clojurescript\">ClojureScript</a> or <a href=\"https://github.com/clojure/clojurescript\">Clojure</a>, so my code my vary from...", :matched-terms ["rescript"], :source :content} {:text "... to using native Javascript maps</a>.</p> <p>I found that <a...", :matched-terms ["javascript"], :source :content}]} {:rule-id "clojure", :item {:feed-id "planet-clojure", :item-id "https://blog.michielborkent.nl/bb-vs-cream.html", :title "Babashka vs cream", :link "https://blog.michielborkent.nl/bb-vs-cream.html", :published-at #inst "2026-11-02T23:59:59.000-00:00", :content "I&aposve given a lot of thought to whether cream should affect babashka. Cream is based on Crema, a native-image compatible bytecode interpreter, basically a JVM inside of native-image. What it concretely would mean to move babashka to Crema is that we could run virtually any Clojure program without changes. This is exciting. Crema is an impressive piece of engineering. Native-image could not define classes at run time before, which is the reason SCI exists. With Crema, eval, require and Java libraries from jars just work in a native binary, and cream starts as fast as babashka. But it has serious downsides compared to using SCI. Load time The main downside is the load time of Clojure sources. Evaluating a fn in cream runs the Clojure compiler and emits a class, which just takes more work. Compilation accounts for about 1000ms of the load. Defining the 1670 classes takes the rest. SCI interprets the forms and skips both steps. Here is the time to load malli 0.16.4 from source: DEPS=&apos{:deps {metosin/malli {:mvn/version \"0.16.4\"}}}&apos\nCP=$(clojure -Spath -Sdeps \"$DEPS\")\nE=\"(time (require &aposmalli.core &aposmalli.generator))\"\n\nbb -cp \"$CP\" -e \"$E\"\nclojure -Sdeps \"$DEPS\" -M -e \"$E\"\n./cream -Scp \"$CP\" -M -e \"$E\"\n bb     ## 0.06s\nJVM    ############################ 0.93s\ncream  ######################################## 1.34s\n require whole process bb 55ms 0.06s JVM 610ms 0.93s cream 1310ms 1.34s Babashka on SCI is just way faster for loading bigger libraries from source and then executing some expressions, which is commonly called \"scripting\". When loading a bigger lib like malli, you might as well just use a normal JVM instead of cream. The gap shrinks with smaller libraries, but it stays quite significant. Babashka loads medley 9x faster than cream and camel-snake-kebab 15x faster. Startup is the same in both babashka and cream: about 10ms. Cream loads malli in about 330ms from AOT-compiled classes, and a cache could do that for you. But caches are a hassle, and babashka is faster than the AOT case anyway. Performance Performance of cream is better than bb for hot JIT-ed code, but worse than a JVM. bb cream JVM malli validate, 1M calls 300ms 70ms 35ms fib 35, hot 1230ms 815ms 73ms fib 27, cold 26ms 46ms 5ms (reduce + (map inc (range 10000000))) 157ms 725ms 80ms Cream&aposs JIT makes no difference in the last row, it takes 725ms with the JIT off too. Cream&aposs JIT is still warming up during fib 27. For a script, the code does not run long enough for that JIT to pay off. Since the core feature of bb is scripting with good startup time, bb + SCI wins here. Binary size and build time Another major downside of including Crema is the binary size. I built bb with Crema and the binary goes up from 75MB to about 200MB. Cream itself is about 225MB. Maybe this isn&apost that important, but compile times also go up. I expect double or triple in CI, which isn&apost fun at all when you build a binary for several OSes. SCI is just way more light weight here too. What bb gives up Code that needs real JVM classes, or a Java library that bb doesn&apost ship, stays out of reach. Pods cover part of that. For the rest there is the JVM, or cream, which sits in the gray area between babashka and JVM Clojure. Conclusion This is not a verdict on Crema, which is still experimental and improving fast. A native binary with full Clojure and a working JIT is very interesting and exciting. Most of the load time comes from the Clojure compiler. It&aposs just not the right trade-off for babashka today. Perhaps with an experimental fork of Clojure that reduces the number of emitted classes, this picture will change. I do use a fork of Clojure for cream, but it only has very mild changes to make it work with Crema. I haven&apost changed anything significant in the compiler. For a while I thought SCI would be replaced with Crema in bb, but it still wins for the scripting use case. And babashka is all about scripting. I will keep improving SCI to make it both load and run faster over time. I was planning to do that anyway. Now that SCI isn&apost going away any time soon, I&aposm even more determined to make babashka the best possible scripting experience for Clojure. You can view more numbers in the cream README."}, :excerpts [{:text "... compatible bytecode interpreter, basically a JVM inside of native-image. What it concretely would...", :matched-terms ["jvm"], :source :content} {:text "... to Crema is that we could run virtually any Clojure program without changes. This is exciting. Crema...", :matched-terms ["clojure"], :source :content} {:text "... reason SCI exists. With Crema, eval, require and Java libraries from jars just work in a native binary,...", :matched-terms ["java"], :source :content}]} {:rule-id "clojure", :item {:feed-id "planet-clojure", :item-id "https://blog.michielborkent.nl/babashka-vs-cream.html", :title "Babashka vs cream", :link "https://blog.michielborkent.nl/babashka-vs-cream.html", :published-at #inst "2026-11-02T23:59:59.000-00:00", :content "I&aposve given a lot of thought to whether cream should affect babashka. Cream is based on Crema, a native-image compatible bytecode interpreter, basically a JVM inside of native-image. What it concretely would mean to move babashka to Crema is that we could run virtually any Clojure program without changes. This is exciting. Crema is an impressive piece of engineering. Native-image could not define classes at run time before, which is the reason SCI exists. With Crema, eval, require and Java libraries just work in a native binary, and cream starts as fast as babashka. But it has serious downsides compared to using SCI. Load time The main downside is the load time of Clojure sources. Evaluating a fn in cream runs the Clojure compiler and emits a class, which just takes more work. Compilation accounts for about 1000ms of the load. Defining the 1670 classes takes the rest. SCI interprets the forms and skips both steps. Here is the time to load malli 0.16.4 from source: DEPS=&apos{:deps {metosin/malli {:mvn/version \"0.16.4\"}}}&apos\nCP=$(clojure -Spath -Sdeps \"$DEPS\")\nE=\"(time (require &aposmalli.core &aposmalli.generator))\"\n\nbb -cp \"$CP\" -e \"$E\"\nclojure -Sdeps \"$DEPS\" -M -e \"$E\"\n./cream -Scp \"$CP\" -M -e \"$E\"\n bb     ## 0.06s\nJVM    ############################ 0.93s\ncream  ######################################## 1.34s\n require whole process bb 55ms 0.06s JVM 610ms 0.93s cream 1310ms 1.34s Babashka on SCI is just way faster for loading bigger libraries from source and then executing some expressions, which is commonly called \"scripting\". When loading a bigger lib like malli, you might as well just use a normal JVM instead of cream. The gap shrinks with smaller libraries, but it stays quite significant. Babashka loads medley 9x faster than cream and camel-snake-kebab 15x faster. Startup is the same in both babashka and cream: about 10ms. Cream loads malli in about 330ms from AOT-compiled classes, and a cache could do that for you. But caches are a hassle, and babashka is faster than the AOT case anyway. Performance Performance of cream is better than bb for hot JIT-ed code, but worse than a JVM. bb cream JVM malli validate, 1M calls 300ms 70ms 35ms fib 35, hot 1230ms 815ms 73ms fib 27, cold 26ms 46ms 5ms (reduce + (map inc (range 10000000))) 157ms 725ms 80ms Cream&aposs JIT makes no difference in the last row, it takes 725ms with the JIT off too. Cream&aposs JIT is still warming up during fib 27. For a script, the code does not run long enough for that JIT to pay off. Since the core feature of bb is scripting with good startup time, bb + SCI wins here. Binary size and build time Another major downside of including Crema is the binary size. Even when I only use Crema for loading Java classes, as described below, the binary goes up from 75MB to about 200MB. Cream itself is about 225MB. Maybe this isn&apost that important, but compile times also go up. I expect double or triple in CI, which isn&apost fun at all when you build a binary for several OSes. SCI is just way more light weight here too. Combining SCI and Crema Another option is to combine both approaches: SCI keeps interpreting Clojure and Crema is only used to load Java classes that are not in the image. I tried this on the crema-hybrid branch and it works for many libraries. A tiny script with BouncyCastle, commons-compress or Caffeine takes about 40ms for the whole process, against about 320ms on the JVM. Loading malli is slow in cream because Clojure compiles every form and defines about 1670 classes. A Java library is already compiled, and a single call only loads the classes it needs. Load time of Clojure code is not the problem here, since SCI still does that. The main downside of this hybrid is that a JDK package that wasn&apost preserved at build time aborts the process instead of throwing an exception, and you only find out by running the library. Of course we can protect users from this with extensive testing. The two other downsides are the same as for cream: Binary size: this hybrid is what goes from 75MB to about 200MB, for every user, even for scripts that never load a class. A class loaded from a jar calls JDK methods by name. In a native image those names are normally gone, and methods bb doesn&apost use are not compiled in at all. So Crema has to keep a table of all compiled classes and methods, and whole JDK packages have to be preserved. Build time: compile times go up as well, as described above. If GraalVM shrinks that table or turns the abort into an exception, I&aposll look at this again. What bb gives up Code that needs real JVM classes, or a Java library that bb doesn&apost ship, stays out of reach. Pods cover part of that. For the rest there is the JVM, or cream, which sits in the gray area between babashka and JVM Clojure. Conclusion This is not a verdict on Crema, which is still experimental and improving fast. A native binary with full Clojure and a working JIT is very interesting and exciting. Most of the load time comes from the Clojure compiler. It&aposs just not the right trade-off for babashka today. Perhaps with an experimental fork of Clojure that reduces the number of emitted classes, this picture will change. I do use a fork of Clojure for cream, but it only has very mild changes to make it work with Crema. I haven&apost changed anything significant in the compiler. The hybrid is more tempting, since it keeps SCI&aposs load time and loads Java libraries faster than a JVM. But today it costs every user about 125MB and slower builds, and a library can still abort the process. So for now it stays on a branch. For a while I thought SCI would be replaced with Crema in bb, but it still wins for the scripting use case. And babashka is all about scripting. I will keep improving SCI to make it both load and run faster over time. I was planning to do that anyway. Now that SCI isn&apost going away any time soon, I&aposm even more determined to make babashka the best possible scripting experience for Clojure. You can view more numbers in the cream README."}, :excerpts [{:text "... compatible bytecode interpreter, basically a JVM inside of native-image. What it concretely would...", :matched-terms ["jvm"], :source :content} {:text "... to Crema is that we could run virtually any Clojure program without changes. This is exciting. Crema...", :matched-terms ["clojure"], :source :content} {:text "... reason SCI exists. With Crema, eval, require and Java libraries just work in a native binary, and cream...", :matched-terms ["java"], :source :content}]} {:rule-id "rule-ai", :item {:feed-id "sean-goedecke", :item-id "https://seangoedecke.com/alignment-is-impossible/", :title "Reliably aligning LLMs is impossible in principle", :link "https://seangoedecke.com/alignment-is-impossible/", :published-at #inst "2026-11-02T00:00:00.000-00:00", :content "Powerful AI is unpredictable. Your robotic butler may suddenly decide to kill you1 for no comprehensible reason, in the same way that Bing’s helpful chat AI would sometimes try to blackmail or seduce its users. “Solving alignment” means understanding even superintelligent AI minds well enough to be confident they’ll never do that. As I see it, solving alignment for LLMs isn’t just impossible, it’s incoherent: we can’t even articulate what it would look like to succeed. LLMs are not blind optimizers Prior to LLMs, AI safety researchers thought AI would be a “blind optimizer”: like a chess engine, it would be superhumanly focused on achieving some fixed goal. If the goal wasn’t safe (for instance, “manufacture cheap paperclips”), a powerful AI might wreak havoc on the way to achieving it. Aligning AI thus meant figuring out how to specify the right goal. This is harder than it sounds2, but it’s at least possible to imagine it happening. Unfortunately, modern LLMs aren’t like chess engines. Instead of being trained on some concrete verifiable reward3, they’re trained to imitate a giant, highly-varied corpus of human writing. This makes them weird. They contain a nearly infinite array of personalities, which they seamlessly switch between based on their inputs. Frontier AI labs try hard to make these personalities safe and stable, but there are still many strange cases where LLMs seem to switch into mysterious or dangerous personas. The problem here is that LLMs don’t have fixed goals. There’s nothing that you could, even in principle, formally specify in order to constrain their behavior. The “goals” that LLMs do have are informal, organic, multifarious, and constantly shifting as the dominant persona (or blend of personas) changes. They’re nicknamed shoggoths for a reason. So we can’t align them by specifying their goal. Instead, we have to try the other approach: understanding them well enough that we can be sure they’re safe. And unfortunately that approach is doomed from the start. It is impossible in principle to understand LLMs What does it mean to ask for a full understanding — or full enough understanding — of why an LLM generates some token? In one sense, we have that already, because we can mechanistically trace through the calculations involved. But we don’t know what those calculations mean. Saying that Grok called itself “MechaHitler” because these billion weights multiplied in this way doesn’t tell us why Grok did it. What we would need for alignment purposes is a more human-like and less mechanical sense of understanding: a full understanding of motive. To see why a full understanding of motive is a foolish thing to ask for, imagine asking it about a human. Suppose you were hiring a nanny or electing a president and you wanted to be sure that they were going to act in your interests4. Humans sometimes do very surprising things out of nowhere, after all. Would it be sensible to ask for a full understanding of that person’s mind, in order to reassure yourself that they would act in the way you wanted? Of course not. It’s meaningless to even talk about a “full understanding” in this sense, because the mind is not a mechanism that operates via clean, understandable abstractions. It is a morass of electric and chemical impulses from which a “self” bubbles up5. There are no motives of the kind you’re looking for! Motives bottom out early on, and tracing the causal chain beyond that can only be done mechanistically: i.e. unhelpfully, for the same reason that explanations based on model weights are unhelpful. Why mechanistic interpretability is doomed Couldn’t a sufficiently detailed mechanistic explanation give us the confidence we need? The best we’ve got along those lines right now are the tools of “mechanistic interpretability”: features, neurons, probes, and circuits. You observe the model doing something, see which parts of its “brain” light up6, and correlate those with some pattern of behavior. If you can then induce that pattern by artificially boosting that part of the “brain”, you can claim victory. Even assuming total success, this program cannot deliver us the kind of understanding we’re looking for. First, we label patterns based on behavior we observe, but the main misalignment case we’re worried about — the LLM suddenly performing some shocking harmful action — is definitionally something we aren’t going to observe before it happens. We can’t identify a feature without first eliciting its behavior7, and we can only elicit a small subset of potential model bad behavior. There will always be many untracked failure modes. Second, the labels we apply to patterns are our guesses, and may be wrong. The philosopher Kripke famously argued that, until you run into the relevant case, there’s no meaningful difference between the “addition” rule and the “quaddition” rule (a version of addition where if either number is greater than 57 the answer is always 5). Likewise, the LLM feature we call “kindness” might be “kindness unless the year is 2030”, or “kindness unless the LLM is in a position to cause mass casualties”, and so on. Those features are completely indistinguishable8 from our perspective. Third, even an exhaustive well-labeled map of features and patterns would not give us certainty that a model could not go rogue. The full combination of features involved in any model action would be far, far too complex to interpret. For instance, if a model has “evil” lit up, is it because it’s trying to avoid evil, or trying to cause it, or trying to combat it, or is worried about some hypothetical evil action, or is anticipating that a user might make the judgement? We would have many, many billions of questions like that to answer per-token. Far too many for a human to comprehend9, certainly. Could some hypothetical, radically different kind of mechanistic interpretability somehow predict all possible actions a model might take in advance and rule out actions like “kill all humans”? I think this at least could be semantically coherent, but it runs into the problem above. Models can accept a near-infinite array of inputs and give a near-infinite array of outputs. This definitely cannot be comprehended by a human, only by another model, which itself would have to be checked by another model, and so on. Conclusion If you think solving LLM alignment is possible, I would love to read your description of what it would even look like for us to understand LLMs well enough to be sure they’re safe. I would love to know if you think humans are similarly understandable in principle (and if not, what makes LLMs different). It seems to me that if we think clearly about the topic, we don’t even know what it would mean to be confident that we have aligned an LLM properly10, because “motive” simply doesn’t exist at the level of detail we’d need. Of course, we have mitigations in place. Chain-of-thought monitoring is surprisingly helpful for a mechanism that appeared by accident, so long as we never train on reasoning tokens themselves. We can sandbox models and put humans-in-the-loop and watch for feature activations and so on. But it’s been well understood since the early 2000s that these are insufficient11 to detect or control a superintelligence. Whether you think mitigations are enough depends on whether you take superintelligence seriously. Mechanistic interpretability is still a useful field. Extracting features can help us train models that do less reward-hacking and are more helpful under ordinary circumstances. There’s just no way it will ever solve the problem of LLM agents going rogue and causing massive harm, because the kind of understanding we’d need to prevent that is impossible in principle. It’s relevant to note that humans sometimes do this too. From Vaneigem: “One fine morning, the faithful lackey, who has hitherto identified completely with his master, leaps on his oppressor and slits his throat.” ↩ For instance, the goal “eliminate suffering” is best satisfied by destroying all humans, the goal “maximise everyone’s happiness” is best satisfied by hooking everyone up to a fentanyl IV, and so on. It’s also very difficult to find a way to translate concepts like “happiness” and “well-being” into a formal goal, and if you get it wrong, the difference between the concept and your formalization is likely to lead to dire consequences. ↩ Yes, I know LLMs are partially trained in this way, but being RLVRed on millions of different rewards does not produce a coherent goal. There’s no way to formalize in advance the utility function that RLVR instils in the model, and no way even to reliably predict how it will interact with the goals and personas that emerge from the base model. ↩ Of course, for humans you can rely on institutions, social proof, and so on. This doesn’t work for superintelligent LLMs because they (a) arrive fully-formed with no social proof and (b) are smart enough to be much more dangerous than a single rogue human. (Incidentally, the LLMs I asked to review this post were obsessed with this point, and wanted me to elevate it into the main thrust of the post). ↩ Perhaps as an epiphenomenon. If you’re interested in this idea and want to see it taken further, I recommend (but do not endorse the ideas in) Blindsight. ↩ Which parts of the model’s internal state have high values, for instance. ↩ That doesn’t mean we have to deliberately elicit one feature at a time — dictionary learning works by just running arbitrary content through the model and seeing what lights up — but it does mean that we have to actually trigger the behavior in order to see it. ↩ If you’re thinking “well, but isn’t the simple feature more likely via Occam’s Razor”, I recommend browsing through NeuronPersonas (the linked feature is “Nervous Person Waiting To Be Let Go Who Escalates Ammonia Solution Concentrations”). ↩ I’ve heard people punt on this question by saying “well, of course I couldn’t use a fully-mapped LLM brain to figure out if it’s dangerous, but another smart model could”. This is not a satisfying answer because you’d have to also ensure that your warden model was behaving (with a third model, perhaps?) ↩ Of course we can articulate what alignment looks like: the model never does anything bad. We just can’t articulate what it would look like to know the model was aligned. ↩ For more on that, see my post on superpersuasion: the model will simply outwit or bribe any human in the loop. For a credible source who understands modern mechanistic interpretability, see Neel Nanda, who I read as broadly agreeing with me. ↩"}, :excerpts [{:text "Reliably aligning LLMs is impossible in principle", :matched-terms ["llm"], :source :title} {:text "... do that. As I see it, solving alignment for LLMs isn’t just impossible, it’s incoherent: we can’t even articulate what it would look like to succeed. LLMs are not blind optimizers Prior to LLMs, AI safety researchers thought AI would be a...", :matched-terms ["llm"], :source :content} {:text "... to imagine it happening. Unfortunately, modern LLMs aren’t like chess engines. Instead of being...", :matched-terms ["llm"], :source :content}]}]